Microsoft's Defender Security Research team described on 2026-05-07 two flaws in Semantic Kernel, Microsoft's open-source framework for building AI agents, both of which turn a prompt injection into an action on the host ([Microsoft Defender Security Research, 2026-05-07](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/07/prompts-become-shells-rce-vulnerabilities-ai-agent-frameworks/)). The maintainers had already published both advisories in February.

CVE-2026-26030 (CVSS 9.9) is in the Python SDK: the default filter of the in-memory vector store is a Python lambda executed with `eval()`, a model-controlled parameter reaches it unsanitised, and the blocklist meant to stop dangerous names can be bypassed, so an attacker who can inject a prompt into an agent with a search plugin backed by that store can run operating-system commands ([Microsoft Defender Security Research, 2026-05-07](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/07/prompts-become-shells-rce-vulnerabilities-ai-agent-frameworks/)). It is fixed in semantic-kernel 1.39.4, and the advisory's workaround is to avoid the in-memory vector store in production ([Microsoft (Semantic Kernel), 2026-02-19](https://github.com/microsoft/semantic-kernel/security/advisories/GHSA-xjw9-4gw8-4rqx)).

CVE-2026-25592 (CVSS 9.9) is in the .NET SDK's sessions Python plugin: a file-download function that writes into a local path was exposed to the model as a callable function, so a model steered by an injected prompt could create a script inside the Azure Container Apps sandbox and then write it to any path on the host, such as the Windows Startup folder, where it runs at the next sign-in ([Microsoft Defender Security Research, 2026-05-07](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/07/prompts-become-shells-rce-vulnerabilities-ai-agent-frameworks/)). It is fixed in the .NET packages 1.71.0 and in the Python package 1.39.3, and the advisory's workaround is a function-invocation filter that allow-lists the local paths those functions may use ([Microsoft (Semantic Kernel), 2026-02-06](https://github.com/microsoft/semantic-kernel/security/advisories/GHSA-2ww3-72rp-wpp4)).

**Exposure:** applications built on Semantic Kernel Python before 1.39.4 that use the in-memory vector store behind a search plugin, or Semantic Kernel .NET before 1.71.0 that load the sessions Python plugin, where untrusted content can reach the model ([Microsoft (Semantic Kernel), 2026-02-19](https://github.com/microsoft/semantic-kernel/security/advisories/GHSA-xjw9-4gw8-4rqx); [Microsoft (Semantic Kernel), 2026-02-06](https://github.com/microsoft/semantic-kernel/security/advisories/GHSA-2ww3-72rp-wpp4)).

**Detection:** Microsoft points to an AI agent process suddenly spawning command lines or dropping scripts into Startup folders ([Microsoft Defender Security Research, 2026-05-07](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/07/prompts-become-shells-rce-vulnerabilities-ai-agent-frameworks/)).

**Defender takeaway:** inventory in-house agents built on Semantic Kernel and confirm their package versions; the fixes have been available since February.
